{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"piecemeal-batiste-greatargus","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Problem\n\nTwo gates make incompatible demands on a ticket description:\n\n1. **The close gate** requires every `[non-codebase]` acceptance criterion to carry an indented\n   structured continuation line:\n   `provenance: environment=<v>; principal=<v>; privilege_posture=<...>; instrument=<...> — <justification>`\n2. **Plan-review admission** rejects a description over **8,000 characters**, which makes the\n   ticket unclaimable.\n\nA ticket with several externally-evidenced criteria cannot satisfy both. Each provenance line costs\nroughly 130-200 characters; nine of them is ~1,200-1,800.\n\n## Observed\n\nClosing `darkish-enormous-raptors` (`76b2-4b5f-5682-45e5`), which had **9** `[non-codebase]`\ncriteria:\n\n- description at **7,989** chars (already near the cap after an earlier gate demanded provenance\n  detail be added);\n- adding the nine required provenance lines projected to roughly **9,070** chars;\n- that is over the admission cap, so the ticket would have become unclaimable — trading a close\n  failure for a worse failure.\n\nThe refusal is not wrong on its own terms. `[non-codebase]` without provenance IS attestation\nlaundering, and the cap protects review quality. Neither gate can see the other's constraint.\n\n## Worked around, not solved\n\nI moved the plan narrative verbatim into a ticket comment, leaving a 4,440-char description\ncarrying all 14 acceptance criteria plus the nine provenance lines. Nothing was lost, and the close\nproceeded.\n\nThat workaround has real costs, which is why this is filed rather than shrugged off:\n\n- **Each description edit stales the plan-review attestation**, forcing a full agentic re-review.\n  This ticket took FOUR review cycles to close, three of them caused purely by formatting demands\n  rather than by anything about the plan.\n- **The plan and its evidence end up in different places.** A later reader sees a description that\n  no longer contains the reasoning, and must know to look in comments.\n- **It is not discoverable.** Nothing tells an author the cap exists until a review is rejected,\n  and nothing tells them provenance lines will consume their remaining budget.\n\nAn independent session hit the same cap the same day authoring `aphidious-florid-grison` and\nresolved it the same way — moving evidence tables into a comment. Two independent hits in one day.\n\n## Relationship to `bediasite-bilineate-stoat`\n\nSame shape, different pair: there, the completion gate demanded a `file_impact` widening that the\nplan-review gate then treated as staling. That was resolved by making the first gate's message\nname the consequence. This one cannot be fixed by wording alone, because the two demands are\ngenuinely incompatible above a certain number of external criteria — no ordering of steps satisfies\nboth.\n\n## Directions (not a prescription)\n\n- Exclude `provenance:` continuation lines from the character count. They are gate-mandated\n  boilerplate, not authored plan content, so counting them against a budget meant to bound review\n  scope is arguably a category error.\n- Or allow provenance to live in a ticket comment keyed to the criterion, which is where the\n  evidence itself already lives.\n- Or raise the cap when tagged criteria are present, proportional to their count.\n- Or have the close gate detect the collision and say so, rather than issuing a demand that cannot\n  be met — the minimum viable fix, and the one most in keeping with how `stoat` was resolved.\n\nWhichever is chosen, an author should not have to discover the conflict by hitting both walls in\nsequence.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] A ticket with enough `[non-codebase]` criteria that the required provenance lines would\n      exceed the admission cap can be closed without moving content out of the description —\n      verified by a test constructing exactly that ticket.\n- [ ] Whichever mechanism is chosen, adding a gate-mandated provenance line does NOT by itself\n      stale a plan-review attestation, since it is boilerplate the gate demanded rather than an\n      author's change of plan — verified by a test.\n- [ ] The collision is detectable before it is hit: an author is told the projected length will\n      exceed the cap at the point the provenance lines are demanded, not after a rejected review.\n- [ ] `make lint`, `make typecheck`, and `python scripts/check_complexity_baseline.py --check`\n      (`new=0 increased=0 stale=0`) pass.","id":"db74-a0a5-2211-4c65","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"Close gate's provenance requirement and plan-review's 8000-char admission cap are mutually unsatisfiable"},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1787287735803500001,"uuid":"5cd714f4-2e49-4418-b86b-4d033269fb7b"}